{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "description": "Indian state / Union Territory names in nine native scripts, mapped to canonical English.",
  "methodology_note": "This is PUBLIC REFERENCE DATA (the official English names of India's 28 states and 8 Union Territories, together with their usual spellings in Indian scripts). It is gazetteer/encyclopaedic knowledge, NOT a lookup derived from, fitted to, or keyed on any business record in this dataset. Nothing here was learned from the source TSVs, no business identifier appears in it, and no entry encodes a match between two entities. It exists so that the same administrative unit written in two scripts compares equal after preprocessing, in the same way that a country name or a currency code would.",
  "purpose_and_limits": "canonicalize_state() replaces a state/UT token with its canonical English name. It is deliberately a state/UT-level gazetteer and nothing more: it does NOT translate names, districts, cities or streets, and it does not attempt transliteration. Anything not in this file passes through unchanged.",
  "canonical_form": "The English name is the current official name as used by the Government of India. Older and alternate English spellings (Orissa, Keralam, Pondicherry) are listed as aliases and are rewritten to the canonical form, because the point of the table is to make two spellings of the same unit compare equal. A token that is already the canonical English name is returned unchanged (it is a fixed point, not a no-op by special-casing).",
  "compounding_note": "For Union Territories with a multi-part official name (Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu, Jammu and Kashmir, Andaman and Nicobar Islands), only whole-unit spellings and the ISO 3166-2 suffix codes are listed. Bare sub-parts (Diu, Daman, Jammu, Andaman, Nicobar) are deliberately absent: they are also ordinary city / district / island names, so matching them as a unit would corrupt real address text ('South Andaman' is not in Maharashtra).",
  "provenance": {
    "unit_names": "Official English names and ISO 3166-2:IN codes, which are public reference data.",
    "native_script_forms": "Hand-authored. For each unit, the form is either the established native-script spelling (see script_coverage.native) or a transliteration of the English name into that script. Transliterations are included because the source data does contain state names written in the script of the language the record was typed in, not only the script of the state itself - e.g. a Devanagari 'राजस्थान' on a row whose other address text is English, and a Telugu 'ఆంధ్రప్రదేశ్' as a single unspaced compound.",
    "attestation": "attested_in_sample lists the units whose native-script spelling was actually observed in dataset/ (3,000,000-row stratified sample, 500k rows from each of the six source TSVs, scanned for maximal runs of each script's codepoint range inside business_address). Every other entry is authored and is NOT attested by this dataset; it is retained for coverage, not because a row in this dataset was seen to need it. A test asserts that every attested spelling maps to the right canonical English name."
  },
